<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>. </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Final Match (FM) scores averaged over five independent trials on each of the four datasets (higher is better).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Evaluation Metrics</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> and baselines by comparing their predicted action sequences against ground-truth action trajectories. For <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span>, this end-to-end evaluation reflects both decision graph quality and execution performance, since any structural error in the graph will lead to execution failure. While <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> includes a separate pipeline for analyzing decision graph quality (see supplementary material for details), we report only end-to-end results here for fairness, as some baselines do not include an explicit structuring stage. Four metrics are reported <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fujisawa et al., 2024</a>)</cite>: (i) Prefix Match Length (PML): longest correct prefix; (ii) Prefix Accuracy (PA): that prefix length normalized by sequence length; (iii) Sequential Match (SM): exact 0/1 match of the full sequence; and (iv) Final Match (FM): 0/1 correctness of the last action. PML and PA measure how long a method stays aligned before drifting, while SM and FM capture end-to-end success. Together these provide a fine-grained view of partial vs. complete correctness. Full mathematical definitions of these metrics are presented below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">3.2.1. </span><span id="S3.SS2.SSS1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> Evaluation Metrics</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T=(t_{1},\dots,t_{N})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T=(t_{1},\dots,t_{N})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the target sequence of length <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P=(p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{M})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P=(p_{1},p_{2},\dots,p_{M})</annotation></semantics></math> denote the predicted sequence of length <math id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math>. We report:</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prefix Match Length (PML):</span>
This metric measures the length of the longest prefix where the predicted and target sequences exactly match. Formally, let <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> be the largest index such that <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t_{i}=p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t_{i}=p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\leq i\leq k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\leq i\leq k</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PML}(T,P)=\max\left(k\mid t_{i}=p_{i}\text{ for all }1\leq i\leq k\right)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>PML</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∣</mo><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> for all </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PML}(T,P)=\max\left(k\mid t_{i}=p_{i}\text{ for all }1\leq i\leq k\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prefix Accuracy (PA):</span>
Using the PML, Prefix Accuracy normalizes the match length by the longer of the two sequences:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{PA}(T,P)=\frac{\text{PML}(T,P)}{\max(N,M)}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>PA</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mtext>PML</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{PA}(T,P)=\frac{\text{PML}(T,P)}{\max(N,M)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">This penalizes both over- and under-prediction, making it robust to output length variation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sequential Match (SM):</span>
A binary indicator of exact match across the full predicted and ground truth sequences:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{SM}(T,P)=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }\text{PA}(T,P)=1,\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>SM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mtext>PA</mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{SM}(T,P)=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }\text{PA}(T,P)=1,\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Final Match (FM):</span>
A binary metric indicating whether the final predicted element matches the final target element, regardless intermediate discrepancies:</p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{FM}(T,P)=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }t_{N}=p_{M},\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>FM</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mtext>otherwise</mtext></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{FM}(T,P)=\begin{cases}1&amp;\text{if }t_{N}=p_{M},\\
0&amp;\text{otherwise}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3. </span>Baselines</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> against baseline methods representative of current approaches to natural language task planning and execution. For fairness, all baselines and <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> use GPT-4, share the same tool library, and are evaluated on the same task queries. While baselines operate directly on unstructured procedure text, <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> first converts it into a decision graph.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I3" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Act-Only</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: Directly generates environment actions (e.g., navigation commands, API calls) from observations, with no explicit intermediate reasoning.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ReAct</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2023</a>)</cite>: Interleaves short reasoning statements with actions, enabling adaptive in-flight planning and interpretable decision paths.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Chain-of-Thought (CoT)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2022</a>)</cite>: Produces a full natural language reasoning chain before outputting actions. Unlike ReAct, reasoning is offline and does not interact with the environment during inference.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I3.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SPRING (Studying the Paper and Reasoning to Play Games)</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024b</a>)</cite>: A zero-shot language agent that combines document understanding with structured reasoning to guide action selection. It first extracts knowledge from task documentation, then uses a manually crafted graph of interdependent questions to reason over the graph in topological order, and finally translates the final answer into an executable action.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Note that we did not include AgentKit <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> as a baseline, since it requires users to manually decompose tasks, construct node-level graphs, and craft prompts for each node. Such extensive engineering is impractical for large-scale evaluation. In contrast, <span id="S3.SS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> generates the entire graph automatically from raw procedures, eliminating this bottleneck.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Results and Analysis</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">End-to-end execution results across all tasks are summarized over five independent runs for each dataset (Table <a href="#S4.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 4. Results and Analysis ‣ PADME: Procedure Aware DynaMic Execution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3.1. Datasets and Benchmarks ‣ 3. Experiments ‣ PADME: Procedure Aware DynaMic Execution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> shows the FM scores. For <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span>, end-to-end results capture both the quality of graph structuring in the <em id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Teach</em> phase and execution in the <em id="S4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Execute</em> phase, providing a fair comparison to baselines that operate directly from free-form procedures.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the <span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recipe</span> dataset, <span id="S4.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> outperforms all four baselines with the average PML being <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.64\pm 0.28" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.64</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.28</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.64\pm 0.28</annotation></semantics></math>, more than doubling the best competitor (SPRING at <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.96\pm 0.04" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.96</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.04</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.96\pm 0.04</annotation></semantics></math>), while PA is <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.69\pm 0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.69</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.07</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.69\pm 0.07</annotation></semantics></math> versus <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.22\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.22</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.22\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> for the strongest baseline. Recipes must be executed in the exact written order as one cannot bake before mixing, therefore, SM is an important metric. <span id="S4.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span>’s SM score of <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.64\pm 0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.64</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.64\pm 0.08</annotation></semantics></math> outerperforms the baselines’ SM. Finally, <span id="S4.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> achieves an FM score of <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.87\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.87</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.87\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> compared with best baseline (<math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.31\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.31</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.31\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> for ReAct).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="S4.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Results are summarized over 5 independent trials. <span id="S4.T1.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> achieves consistently high performance across all datasets.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T1.6" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T1.6.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.6.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<td id="S4.T1.6.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" colspan="4">Metric</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.6.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.6.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row">Method</th>
<td id="S4.T1.6.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">PML</td>
<td id="S4.T1.6.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">PA</td>
<td id="S4.T1.6.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">SM</td>
<td id="S4.T1.6.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">FM</td></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.6.3" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.6.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t" colspan="5"><span id="S4.T1.6.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Recipe Dataset</span></th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T1.6.4" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T1.6.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_t">Act-Only</th>
<td id="S4.T1.6.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.87\pm 0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.87</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.87\pm 0.06</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="S4.T1.6.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S4.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.22\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.22</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.22\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math></td>
