<th id="S4.T1.6.26.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T1.6.26.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> (ours)</th>
<td id="S4.T1.6.26.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T1.6.26.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">5.15 <math id="S4.T1.m77" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.07</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.6.26.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T1.6.26.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.71 <math id="S4.T1.m78" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.03</span></td>
<td id="S4.T1.6.26.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.30 <math id="S4.T1.m79" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.06</td>
<td id="S4.T1.6.26.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S4.T1.6.26.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">0.74 <math id="S4.T1.m80" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> 0.01</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In <span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScienceWorld</span>, <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALFWorld</span>, and <span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Business Process</span> datasets, FM reflects whether a task is successfully completed. However, FM alone can be misleading, since different methods may reach the same final action through divergent or error-prone trajectories. To assess procedural reliability, FM must be considered together with trajectory-based metrics (PML, PA, SM), which capture how consistently an agent stays aligned with the intended procedure. Furthermore, there exists more than one correct sequence of actions that can complete a task, it is possible for a method to achieve high FM, while having lower match scores PML, PA, and SM, which compare against a single reference trajectory. Ideally, all these metrics should be examined together to provide a more complete picture of performance.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">On the <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Business Process</span> dataset, <span id="S4.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> achieves PML of <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.15\pm 0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.15</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.07</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.15\pm 0.07</annotation></semantics></math> beating the best baseline (C-o-T’s <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.42\pm 0.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.42</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.42\pm 0.24</annotation></semantics></math>) and PA is <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.71\pm 0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.71</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.71\pm 0.03</annotation></semantics></math> compared to ReAct and C-o-T’s PA score of <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.52\pm 0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.52</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.52\pm 0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, while SM is <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.30\pm 0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.30</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.30\pm 0.06</annotation></semantics></math> because execution order is this dataset is flexible. <span id="S4.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> attains an FM of <math id="S4.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.74\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.74</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.74\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math>. We observe that C-o-T achieves an FM score (<math id="S4.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.74\pm 0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.74</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.74\pm 0.06</annotation></semantics></math>) close to <span id="S4.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span>, but its lower PML, PA, and SM indicate that it is less consistent in following the intended trajectory. This suggests that C-o-T can sometimes reach the correct final action, but with less stable alignment to the procedure, whereas <span id="S4.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> maintains stronger trajectory fidelity.
On the <span id="S4.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ALFWorld</span> dataset, <span id="S4.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> consistently outperforms the baselines across all metrics, achieving a PML of <math id="S4.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.31\pm 0.06" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.31</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.06</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.31\pm 0.06</annotation></semantics></math>, PA of <math id="S4.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.74\pm 0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.74</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.74\pm 0.02</annotation></semantics></math>, SM of <math id="S4.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.62\pm 0.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.62</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.02</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.62\pm 0.02</annotation></semantics></math>, and FM of <math id="S4.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.69\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.69</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.69\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math>. On the <span id="S4.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ScienceWorld</span> dataset, <span id="S4.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> achieves a PML of <math id="S4.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.49\pm 0.07" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.49</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.07</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.49\pm 0.07</annotation></semantics></math> against the best baseline’s <math id="S4.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.05\pm 0.08" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.05</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.08</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.05\pm 0.08</annotation></semantics></math>, and its plans match the reference more often (PA is <math id="S4.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.44\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.44</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.44\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> vs. Act-Only’s <math id="S4.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.36\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.36</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.36\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math>). The SM score is slightly lower <math id="S4.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.16\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.16</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.16\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.18\pm 0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.18</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.18\pm 0.01</annotation></semantics></math> for Act-Only, which is acceptable because many lab tasks let steps be taken in flexible order. Considering all metrics together, <span id="S4.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> delivers more reliable performance overall.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">On deeper inspection, we observe distinct shortcomings across different baselines. ReAct falls into reasoning–action loops (e.g., sensor <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> capture <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> sensor <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> capture), while C-o-T is prone to hallucinations when sketching long plans without grounding. Surprisingly, Act-Only occasionally performs at par with or even better than other baselines; we attribute this to its simplicity, which avoids compounding reasoning errors while navigating through complex procedures, though it lacks robustness across domains. In contrast, <span id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span>’s consistently stronger results across all datasets suggest that the explicit decision graph built during the <span id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Teach</span> phase yields a steadier plan, limiting early drift and cascading errors during execution. Because this graph is pre-computed, the <span id="S4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Execute</span> phase is streamlined. The <em id="S4.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Execute</em> phase follows the graph, executes the prescribed actions and delivers the most stable performance across domains.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the impact of ambiguity, we specifically report results on the <em id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hard</em> category Recipe tasks in Table <a href="#S4.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 4. Results and Analysis ‣ PADME: Procedure Aware DynaMic Execution" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, where the tasks provide only a set of ingredients and the execution agent must infer the intended dish based on the provided ingredients before proceeding with the execution. <span id="S4.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> delivers a clear margin over all baselines with FM reaching <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.81" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.81</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.81</annotation></semantics></math>, nearly triple that of C-o-T and SPRING, while SM climbing to <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.47" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.47</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.47</annotation></semantics></math>. These results highlight that while baselines struggle under ambiguity, the structured decision graph abstraction in <span id="S4.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> enables more stable execution. Since real-world procedural tasks are inherently ambiguous, methods that can accommodate such uncertainty are essential. Notably, all baselines have visibility of the tool library from the outset, whereas <span id="S4.p6.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> first constructs the decision graph purely from procedure text, attaching tools to nodes only afterward based on the graph’s logical structure.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S4.T2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>. </span><span id="S4.T2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance on <em id="S4.T2.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Hard</em> Category Recipe tasks (summarized over 5 independent trials). <span id="S4.T2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">PADME</span> outperforms all baselines, showing strong robustness under ambiguity.</span></figcaption>
<table id="S4.T2.7" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S4.T2.7.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.7.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_tt"></th>
<th id="S4.T2.7.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" colspan="4">Metric</th></tr>
<tr id="S4.T2.7.2" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S4.T2.7.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row"><span id="S4.T2.7.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Method</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.7.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.7.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PML</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.7.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.7.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PA</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.7.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.7.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">SM</span></th>
<th id="S4.T2.7.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t"><span id="S4.T2.7.2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">FM</span></th></tr>
</thead>
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S4.T2.7.3" class="ltx_tr">
